{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"files\": [\n    {\n      \"content\": \"/*\\n    This program solves Laplace's equation on a regular 2D grid using simple Jacobi iteration.\\n\\n    The stencil calculation stops when  ( err >= CONV_THRESHOLD OR  iter > ITER_MAX )\\n*/\\n#include <stdio.h>\\n#include <stdlib.h>\\n#include <time.h>\\n#include <sys/time.h>\\n\\n#define CONV_THRESHOLD 1.0e-5f // threshold of convergence\\n\\n// matrix to be solved\\ndouble **grid;\\n\\n// auxiliary matrix\\ndouble **new_grid;\\n\\n// size of each side of the grid\\nint size;\\nint iter_max_num;\\n\\n// return the absolute value of a number\\ndouble absolute(double num){\\n    if(num < 0)\\n        return -1.0 * num;\\n    return num;\\n}\\n\\n\\n// allocate memory for the grid\\nvoid allocate_memory(){\\n    grid = (double **) malloc(size * sizeof(double *));\\n    new_grid = (double **) malloc(size * sizeof(double *));\\n\\n    for(int i = 0; i < size; i++){\\n        grid[i] = (double *) malloc(size * sizeof(double));\\n        new_grid[i] = (double *) malloc(size * sizeof(double));\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// initialize the grid\\nvoid initialize_grid(){\\n    int center = size / 2;\\n    int linf = center - (size / 10);\\n    int lsup = center + (size / 10);\\n    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++){\\n        for (int j = 0; j < size; j++){\\n            // inicializa região de calor no centro do grid\\n            if ( i >= linf && i <= lsup && j >= linf && j <= lsup)\\n                grid[i][j] = 100;\\n            else\\n                grid[i][j] = 0;\\n            new_grid[i][j] = 0.0;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// save the grid in a file\\nvoid save_grid(){\\n\\n    for(int i = 0; i < size; i++){\\n        for(int j = 0; j < size; j++){\\n            fprintf(stdout, \\\"%lf \\\", grid[i][j]);\\n        }\\n        fprintf(stdout, \\\"\\\\n\\\");\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nint main(int argc, char *argv[]){\\n\\n    fscanf(stdin, \\\"%d\\\", &size);\\n    fscanf(stdin, \\\"%d\\\", &iter_max_num);\\n    \\n    int hasError = 1;\\n    int iter = 0;\\n\\n    struct timeval time_start;\\n    struct timeval time_end;\\n\\n    // allocate memory to the grid (matrix)\\n    allocate_memory();\\n\\n    // set grid initial conditions\\n    initialize_grid();\\n\\n    //printf(\\\"Jacobi relaxation calculation: %d x %d grid\\\\n\\\", size, size);\\n\\n    // get the start time\\n    //gettimeofday(&time_start, NULL);\\n\\n    // Jacobi iteration\\n    // This loop will end if either the maximum change reaches below a set threshold (convergence)\\n    // or a fixed number of maximum iterations have completed\\n    while ( hasError && iter < iter_max_num ) {\\n\\n        hasError = 0;\\n        // calculates the Laplace equation to determine each cell's next value\\n        for( int i = 1; i < size-1; i++) {\\n            const double * restrict current_row = grid[i];\\n            const double * restrict upper_row = grid[i-1];\\n            const double * restrict lower_row = grid[i+1];\\n            double * restrict next_row = new_grid[i];\\n            int row_has_error = 0;\\n\\n            // Rows are separately allocated; only the integer flag is reduced.\\n            #pragma omp simd reduction(|:row_has_error)\\n            for(int j = 1; j < size-1; j++) {\\n                double next_value = 0.25 * (current_row[j+1] + current_row[j-1] +\\n                                            upper_row[j] + lower_row[j]);\\n                next_row[j] = next_value;\\n\\n                double errorCurrent = absolute(next_value - current_row[j]);\\n                row_has_error |= (errorCurrent > CONV_THRESHOLD);\\n            }\\n            hasError |= row_has_error;\\n        }\\n        // copie the next values into the working array for the next iteration\\n        for( int i = 1; i < size-1; i++) {\\n            double * restrict current_row = grid[i];\\n            const double * restrict next_row = new_grid[i];\\n\\n            #pragma omp simd\\n            for( int j = 1; j < size-1; j++) {\\n                current_row[j] = next_row[j];\\n            }\\n        }\\n        iter++;\\n    }\\n\\n    // get the end time\\n    //gettimeofday(&time_end, NULL);\\n\\n    //double exec_time = (double) (time_end.tv_sec - time_start.tv_sec) +\\n    //                   (double) (time_end.tv_usec - time_start.tv_usec) / 1000000.0;\\n\\n    //save the final grid in file\\n    save_grid();\\n\\n    //printf(\\\"\\\\nKernel executed in %lf seconds with %d iterations.\\\\n\\\", exec_time, iter);\\n\\n    return 0;\\n}\\n\",\n      \"path\": \"src/laplace_seq.c\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Restrict-qualified row pointers and an explicit integer OR convergence reduction should enable legal ARM64 SIMD vectorization without changing floating-point evaluation order.\",\n  \"notes\": \"Unmeasured. Preserves initialization, boundaries, copy-back, stopping condition and output formatting. No alignment assumptions or floating-point reassociation. If OpenMP SIMD directives are disabled, the explicit reduction and restrict qualifiers still support automatic vectorization.\",\n  \"research\": {\n    \"queries\": [],\n    \"urls\": []\n  },\n  \"sources_kept\": [],\n  \"transformations\": [\n    \"Expose independently allocated input and output rows through restrict-qualified pointers.\",\n    \"Replace conditional convergence-flag updates with a per-row integer OR reduction and an OpenMP SIMD directive.\",\n    \"Annotate the independent copy-back loop for SIMD while retaining the original stencil arithmetic and two-phase iteration.\"\n  ]\n}"}}
